ading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#forced-disconnect-on-revocation","content":"Forced disconnect on revocation"},"1297":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-22","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#device-shadow-and-automation","content":"Device Shadow and automation"},"1298":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-23","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#what-the-connector-writes-to-the-shadow","content":"What the connector writes to the shadow"},"1299":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-24","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#aws-iot-policies","content":"AWS IoT policies"},"1300":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-25","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#troubleshooting","content":"Troubleshooting"},"1301":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-26","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","content":"Lamassu's integration with AWS IoT Core bridges the PKI managed by Lamassu and AWS's connectivity platform. When a device obtains its certificate through the EST enrollment process, the connector ensures that certificate and its associated identity are recognized in AWS IoT Core without manual intervention. The result is that the device can authenticate directly to AWS with the same X.509 certificate Lamassu issued."},"1302":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-27","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","content":"From a protocol standpoint, the device authenticates to AWS IoT Core via mutual TLS (mTLS): it presents its Lamassu-issued X.509 certificate as the client certificate, and AWS IoT Core verifies the certificate chain up to the CA registered in the account. Lamassu acts as the certification authority (CA); the connector ensures that CA is registered in AWS and that the device's certificate is known to the platform before the device attempts to connect (in `auto` mode) or on its first connection (in JITP mode)."},"1303":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-28","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","content":"This document is aimed both at the PKI administrator, who needs to configure the connector and register CAs, and at the device firmware developer, who needs to understand how Things are named, how the Device Shadow works and what happens when a certificate is revoked. For the firmware developer, the most important fact is that **the AWS Thing name (ThingName) always corresponds to the Common Name (CN) of the certificate issued by Lamassu**, regardless of the provisioning mode."},"1304":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-29","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#connector-architecture","content":"The AWS IoT Core connector is an independent Go process, a binary or container separate from Lamassu's core. In a monolithic deployment it is compiled by default (build tag `!noaws`) and activated with `aws_iot_manager.enabled: true` in the configuration file. In distributed deployments, it runs as its own container with access to Lamassu's CA, DMS Manager and Device Manager services over HTTP."},"1305":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-30","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#connector-architecture","content":"The connector does not poll Lamassu's services periodically. Instead, it **subscribes to Lamassu's internal event bus** (AMQP or AWS SQS/SNS, configurable) and reacts to the events it cares about: CA creation or update, DMS creation or update, device identity binding, certificate status change, certificate metadata update and device metadata update. This event-driven model ensures synchronization with AWS is nearly immediate and produces no polling load."},"1306":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-31","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#connector-architecture","content":"To avoid infinite loops, the connector ignores any event whose CloudEvent `source` field matches its own origin URI. This prevents the metadata writes the connector itself performs from re-triggering the same flow."},"1307":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-32","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#connector-architecture","content":"When several connectors run in parallel, for example for different AWS accounts, each instance has its own `connector_id`. All metadata the connector writes to Lamassu is stored under the key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}`, allowing coexistence without collisions."},"1308":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-33","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#prerequisites","content":"Before enabling the connector you need an AWS account with the proper IAM permissions and the connector correctly configured. The deployment process (Docker Compose, Kubernetes or binary) is described in the deployment documentation."},"1309":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-34","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#required-iam-permissions","content":"The IAM identity operating the connector needs at minimum the following permissions on the target account's resources:"},"1310":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-35","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#required-iam-permissions","content":"IAM action"},"1311":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core-36","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-iot-core#required-iam-permissions","content":"Purpose"},"1312":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-68/platform/iot-fleets/integrations/aws-io