,[2250,"<Card title=\"OCSP service\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />"],[2016,"<Card title=\"Online validation with OCSP\" description=\"Generate a query and verify signature, status and timestamps.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />"],[2454,"<Card title=\"PKI diagnostics\" description=\"Check issuance, OCSP and CRL when the result fails.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/troubleshooting\" />"],[2452,"<Card title=\"PKI getting started\" description=\"Create a CA, then issue and verify a client certificate.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/quickstarts/overview\" />"],[1096,"<Card title=\"Provisioning strategies\" description=\"Choose automatic registration, preregistration or manual assignment.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies\" />"],[1101,"<Card title=\"Renewal and recovery\" description=\"Generate the successor and handle expired or lost credentials.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/renewal-and-recovery\" />"],[864,"<Card title=\"Renewal and recovery\" description=\"Install the successor and handle failures without losing known state.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/renewal-and-recovery\" />"],[2017,"<Card title=\"Revocation lists\" description=\"Download, inspect and use an issuer’s CRL.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/crl\" />"],[1103,"<Card title=\"Troubleshooting\" description=\"Distinguish transport, policy, issuance and installation failures.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting\" />"],[1099,"<Card title=\"Trust distribution\" description=\"Update roots and chains with verified overlap.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/trust-distribution\" />"],[412,"<Card title=\"Upgrade from 3.8.0\" description=\"Upgrade an existing Helm release to 4.0.0: architecture changes, breaking changes, migration steps and validation.\" href=\"/docs/upgrades/3-8-0-to-4-0-0\" />"],[414,"<Card title=\"Use Fastlane\" description=\"Bootstrap a complete evaluation or lab environment with one script.\" href=\"/docs/deployment/self-hosted/fastlane\" />"],[2055,"<Card title=\"Validate with OCSP\" description=\"Query and verify a certificate’s signed status.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />"],[1638,"1 hour."],[202,"1\\. Install the Gateway prerequisites"],[203,"2\\. Create a values file"],[2725,"3.8.0 deployments with per-service databases"],[2802,"3.8.0 did not pin a container identity, so existing files can carry any UID. Any volume that holds keys or CRLs must be owned by `65532:65532`, or at least readable (and, where needed, writable) by that identity."],[2688,"3.8.0 gave each service its own database. The 4.0.0 backend connects to a single PostgreSQL database named `pki` and isolates each service in a schema: `kms`, `ca`, `va`, `devicemanager`, `dmsmanager` and `alerts`. The `authz` service keeps its principals, grants and policies in a separate `authz` database (the development monolith uses an `authz` schema inside `pki` instead), and WFX keeps its own `wfx` database."],[2799,"3.8.0 had no authorization service, so a 3.8.0 values file has nothing to migrate from: you must add the `services.authz` configuration yourself. It needs the JWKS endpoint of your identity provider, the database mappings the authorization engine uses, and a bootstrap principal that matches how your provider assigns the administrator role. Without a matching principal nobody can administer the PKI."],[2636,"3.8.0 to 4.0.0"],[208,"3\\. Install Lamassu"],[1640,"30 minutes / 10 minutes."],[2691,"4.0.0 removes the gateway policy. The `/api/*` routes reach the services directly, and each service authorizes every request through `authz`, which validates the token against the identity provider's JWKS endpoint and applies fine-grained, entity-level policies to CAs, certificates, devices, DMSs, KMS keys and CRL settings. There is no implicit superuser: the first administrator comes from a bootstrap principal that you configure. The access control page documents principals, policies and the first administrator."],[209,"4\\. Verify the deployment"],[1214,"400 `struct validation error` creating or updating a DMS"],[1198,"400, CSR or format error"],[1196,"404 or missing APS"],[2171,"6 days and 23 hours."],[2168,"7 days."],[66,"99.5 %"],[70,"99.9 %"],[74,"99.99 %"],[533,"A **grant** is the association between a principal and a policy. Removing the grant removes all permissions contributed by that policy, without modifying it for other principals."],[530,"A **policy** groups reusable permissions. It can be granted to several principals and can contain rules about Lamassu entities, rules about HTTP routes or both."],[529,"A **principal** connects a credential to Lamassu. It defines how to recognize an OIDC or X.509 identity and can be active or disabled."],[908,"A &#x2A;*Device Management Service (DMS)** configures admission and identity lifecycle for its devices. It acts as a registration authority; the CA selected in `Enrollment CA` signs certificates. This guide is for the policy administrator."],[703,"A `http://` URL shows the console warning `Unencrypted HTTP connection` (see the Credentials over http\\:// callout below)."],[556,"A `skip_authz` route and a `default_action: \"allow\"` prefix grant access without consulting any policy. Every identity that matches an active principal can reach what they open, including identities with no granted policies. A `skip_authz` route in one schema also opens a request that another schema maps to a grant-protected action. Review these settings whenever you add or upgrade an integrated service."],[457,"A 500 indicates an internal failure, such as an unavailable database. The `{\"err\": ...}` body currently includes the text of the internal error, which can contain schema, table and constraint names and, on connection failures, the database host and user (not the password). Find the cause in the service log using the request time, and do not forward the body to third parties or show it to end users. This is a known information-disclosure defect: generic messages are planned for 500 responses, keeping the detail only in the service log (tracked in lamassuiot#814). The authorization connector also returns the internal error, in the `details.error` field. Some request errors, such as a key size the engine does not support, are also returned as 500."],[975,"A body with `config.auth_mode` set to `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls` must carry its corresponding option block (`apikey_options`, `jwt_options` or `mtls_options`) — and nothing else. Bodies with those auth modes carrying only `apikey`, `oidc` or `mtls` fail validation with 400, and `validate_server_cert` is always ignored: use `insecure_skip_verify`. Only bodies without option blocks are accepted as `noauth`, and their credentials are dropped."],[885,"A bodyless `204` does not satisfy the contract. Denial, transport error, uninterpretable response or timeout abort authorization. Combined mode validates the certificate first, then queries the webhook. See the webhook specification."],[1755,"A CA determines who signs, and a profile defines what can be issued. The DMS applies admission and enrollment policy. The device retains its association with certificates, and the external consumer decides which identity it accepts."],[2867,"A CA ID equal to the SKI of another VA role: their CRL URLs would be ambiguous."],[2866,"A CA whose ID equals the `legacy-<ski>` ID the migration reserves for the archived settings of another role whose CA was deleted: recreate that CA with a different ID."],[2180,"A CA whose signer lacks `cRLSign` cannot generate its CRL, and an update that selects a signer without `cRLSign` is rejected (400 error). Since 4.0 the VA requires this usage. Check before importing with `openssl x509 -noout -ext keyUsage` and, if it is missing, obtain a certificate for the same CA and key that includes it and update the settings to use it as the signer. After reissuing a CA, also check the key usage of the new certificate: it 