tory. A consumer trusts an identity when it can build a chain to a root it recognizes, check that the certificate is fit for the intended use and verify that it is still valid."],[1066,"In mTLS mode, the enrollment CA always validates the re-enrollment credential; certificates from other issuers are accepted only if they are listed as validation CAs in the re-enrollment block. The expiry check through `enable_expired_renewal` belongs to that validator. A revoked current certificate and a request before the window are rejected. See Renewal for installation and recovery."],[2277,"In practice, it lets you:"],[656,"In the `authz` logs, an external endpoint decision includes `allowed`, `reason`, `matched_principals`, `evaluated_policy_ids`, `matched_policy_id` and the status code. These fields let you separate a matching failure from a permissions failure. With OpenTelemetry tracing enabled, a request that reaches the policy engine produces an `engine.CheckHTTPRequest` span whose attributes record `authz.allowed`, the matched policy and the matched principal. Correlation with the Gateway trace requires trace context to be propagated to authz."],[1693,"In the authentication section, enable **Require client certificate** and leave **Call external webhook** off. Under **Require client certificate**, add the CA whose initial certificates you will accept to **Validation CAs**; for this evaluation, it can be the same CA. Keep **Verify CSR Signature** enabled and leave **Enable server-side key generation** off if you will not use it."],[2275,"In the current configuration, Lamassu IoT works exclusively with asymmetric keys, such as RSA and elliptic curves. These keys are used to issue X.509 certificates, sign requests and perform authentication and validation operations within the system."],[392,"In the default `tls.type: certManager` mode, cert-manager `Issuer` and CA `Certificate` resources and a chart-managed downstream `Certificate`; in the `tls.type: external` mode, a reference to your TLS Secret"],[2117,"In the device-generated CSR flow, the CA applies the profile and signs without receiving the device’s private key."],[1271,"In the DMS, separate distributed CAs from the re-enrollment validation CAs at `reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas`, used for re-enrollment credentials. That list is optional because the enrollment CA always validates re-enrollment credentials, but when switching the enrollment CA, add the previous issuance CA to it so old-CA identities keep re-enrolling during overlap. The inspected validator tries the enrollment CA followed by those CAs against the received leaf; accepting a multilevel chain requires checking what reaches the service and which issuer is configured."],[1128,"In the inspected code, finding any device owned by the same DMS triggers the `enable_replaceable_enrollment` check before PRE\\_REGISTRATION. With the flag disabled, a preregistered device can receive `forbiddenNewEnrollment` even in **No Identity**. Selecting PRE\\_REGISTRATION alone therefore does not establish a working path."],[1530,"In the policy above, the `<AWS_REGION>` and `<AWS_ACCOUNT_ID>` placeholders must be replaced with the AWS region and the twelve-digit account identifier of the target account before applying the policy. The `${iot:Connection.Thing.ThingName}` variable automatically limits each permission to the Thing the device is connected as, following the principle of least privilege. The `${ShadowName}` variable in the shadow ARNs is a placeholder that must be replaced with the `shadow_config.shadow_name` value configured in the DMS; if the Classic Shadow is used, it can be simplified by removing the `name/${ShadowName}/` segment. AWS IoT policies do not support regex alternation; the `[name/${ShadowName}/]*` notation represents the optionality of that segment within ARN syntax constraints."],[2322,"In the wizard, choose **Import Existing Key Pair** and complete the requested fields:"],[2511,"In this flow, the client's key is generated in the browser and must be downloaded when issuance finishes. If you lose it, you must generate a key and issue another certificate. The certificate inventory lets you download the public certificate, but cannot recover this key."],[1450,"In this mode the connector **creates no Things, groups or policies** in AWS IoT Core. It only syncs certificate status (active, revoked, suspended) as it changes in Lamassu. It is the right mode when Thing provisioning in AWS is managed by an external system and Lamassu only needs to keep certificate status up to date."],[464,"inaccessible image or incorrect pull policy;"],[1646,"Include Enrollment CA enabled; Include Lamassu System CA disabled."],[509,"Included policies"],[322,"Independent Gateways and separate external IPs"],[325,"Independent Gateways on one node and one IP"],[2661,"Independent re-enrollment authentication"],[2792,"Independent re-enrollment authentication"],[1778,"Infrastructure operator"],[4,"Infrastructure requirements"],[1631,"Initial authentication"],[1059,"Initial authentication and admission; server generation enabled."],[1053,"Initial authentication and DMS registration/replacement policy."],[978,"Initial authentication and renewal settings are stored independently. If a DMS only has the initial authentication block, renewal reuses it: read both blocks before editing. The console exposes independent editors for the enrollment and re-enrollment authentication, with the same Log Level, Call Timeout, HTTP Authentication and credential fields as the webhook channels, plus a **Skip TLS Verification** switch (off by default, so verification stays on; the console warns when you enable it)."],[1595,"Initial credential, key, EST client, trust and certificate installation."],[1775,"Initial credential, server trust, EST client, and key and certificate storage."],[930,"Initial enrollment rejects an ID owned by another DMS. Do not automatically enable replacement in response to an error; Troubleshooting distinguishes repeated IDs, ownership and partial changes."],[877,"Initial mTLS validation does not by itself compare bootstrap CN with the requested CSR CN. If a requester should only request its own `Device ID`, add a control binding credential to ID, such as the combined webhook, and test a request with another CN. Certificate re-enrollment does compare its CN to the CSR; subsequent checks use the registered active identity."],[867,"Initial server trust"],[2165,"Initial value"],[740,"input: data received by the operation;"],[812,"Inspect a device"],[1848,"Inspect an authority"],[2501,"Inspect content and dates"],[476,"inspect dead-letter queues;"],[1080,"Inspect the CN, serial, dates and uses; check association in Lamassu before installing. Protect the DER and PEM files containing the key and remove temporary copies according to the device's storage mechanism. See openssl-pkcs8 and the Python MIME parser."],[1181,"Inspect the device, issued certificates and history before retrying. A successor may have been issued or the previous identity revoked."],[446,"inspect the token's real claims;"],[2019,"Inspect, download and revoke the certificates issued by Lamassu."],[873,"Install a previously verified root, chain or pin through a trusted channel: manufacturing, approved configuration or authenticated distribution. It enables TLS and server-name verification before downloading `/cacerts`."],[1151,"Install and check the new pair"],[513,"Install and verify administrative access"],[226,"Install cert-manager separately before Lamassu, but only for the default `tls.type: certManager` mode. In that mode the chart renders its self-signed Issuer and CA Certificate resources regardless of whether `tls.certManagerOptions.clusterIssuer` is set, so cert-manager stays a prerequisite. With `tls.type: external`, the chart renders no cert-manager resources and the Gateway terminates TLS with an existing Secret you provide instead."],[2044,"Install certificate and chain in the intended consumer and test a connection. Issuance alone does not register a device or grant access in an integration."],[170,"Install lab HSM components and configure KMS for PKCS#11"],[200,"Install Lamassu with Helm"],[224,"Install or upgrade the Envoy Gateway CRDs before its controller. The commands below match the version used by the Lamassu Helm repository:"],[855,"Install the key/certificate pair and required intermediates on the device and test usage."],[1249,"Install the required material"],[161,"Install Victoria Logs, VictoriaTraces, Jaeger and an OpenTelemetry Collector"],[198,"Install with Helm"],[1779,"Installation, network, availability, dependencies and deployment diagnosis."],[2749,"Installations whose CA keys predate the KMS"],[1233,"Installed pair, chain, uses, consumer trust and permissions. Also check the slot and history."],[186,"installs Keycloak and creates the `lamassu` realm, frontend client, admin role and initial user"],[183,"installs or upgrades Envoy Gateway 1.8 and creates the `eg` `GatewayClass`"],[185,"installs PostgreSQL and creates the required databases"],[187,"installs RabbitMQ"],[2618,"Instead, report privately using GitHub Security Advisories for the affected repository:"],[907,"Integrate the devices"],[1011,"Integrate the EST client"],[497,"Integrated or external HTTP services"],[1561,"Integrations"],[471,"Internal calls now fail fast instead of hanging for the clients covered by Egress proxy: connection attempts time out after 30 seconds and TLS handshakes after 10 seconds. The authorization-engine connector keeps its own transport without these dial and handshake timeouts; only its overall 30-second request timeout bounds each call. If an egress proxy handles the cluster's outbound traffic, check the pod's `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables and list in-cluster service names in `NO_PROXY`."],[344,"Internal Lamassu clients — the Vault engine connection, service-to-service calls and webhook deliveries — honor the `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables. The exception is the authorization-engine connector: it uses its own transport and does not consult these proxy variables. If your deployment routes egress through a proxy, list in-cluster service names in `NO_PROXY` (for example `.svc,.cluster.local`) so internal traffic stays inside the cluster. Loopback traffic is never sent through a proxy."],[465,"invalid cryptographic engine configuration;"],[1666,"Inventory"],[1787,"Inventory the dependencies"],[1556,"IoT Fleets"],[1563,"IoT Fleets applies PKI to the device lifecycle: registration, obtaining an identity, renewal and retirement. It is intended for those who administer a device population and those who integrate its enrollment client or the system that accepts it."],[1751,"IoT Fleets overview"],[2061,"Issuance"],[782,"Issuance and enrollment"],[2576,"Issuance fails"],[902,"Issuance profile"],[1623,"Issuance profile"],[1958,"Issuance through a DMS"],[2058,"Issuance, use, renewal, revocation and retirement of certificates in Lamassu."],[1615,"Issue a bootstrap leaf certificate with `CN=est-device-001`, its own key and Client Authentication use. Follow Issue and verify a certificate, changing its CN and saving the result as `bootstrap.crt` and `bootstrap.key`. Choose validity that covers this test and check the pair and chain."],[2493,"Issue a client certificate and check content, chain and key correspondence."],[1733,"Issue and control certificates"],[2429,"Issue and control certificates"],[2499,"Issue and download"],[2492,"Issue and verify a certificate"],[1849,"Issue certificates"],[2022,"Issue from an existing CSR"],[2135,"Issue from intermediate CAs separated by environment or risk domain."],[2534,"Issuer cannot be found"],[1777,"Issuer trust, certificate validation and permissions in the destination."],[2069,"Issuing a certificate is only the beginning. During its useful life you must check its validity, renew it before it expires and retire it when it stops being trustworthy. Lamassu keeps the certificate, its issuing CA, its status and, when it belongs to a device, the identity it is linked to."],[1622,"Issuing root from `device-root.pem`."],[856,"Issuing, binding and installing are separate steps. If an operation fails, check which steps persisted before repeating it."],[1442,"It calls `iot:GetRegistrationCode` to obtain AWS's verification code."],[775,"it can be retrieved by resource, actor and period;"],[2884,"It covers the VA migrations only: an all-clear does not prove that the CA and KMS migration guards pass, so it does not replace the duplicate CA ID and `engine_id` checks described in PostgreSQL consolidation."],[2567,"It identifies `device-001`, supports client authentication and verifies against `ca.pem`."],[2565,"It is a CA certificate, is currently valid and its self-signature verifies."],[2373,"It requires the module, token and permissions of the corresponding provider. Check that the Lamassu identifier matches the object accessible from that module. Module configuration belongs to the client environment or Deployment."],[1444,"It sends the CA (in PEM) and the verification certificate to AWS via `iot:RegisterCACertificate`, enabling registration with `AllowAutoRegistration: true`."],[542,"It separately loads the policies granted to each principal."],[2448,"its chain reaches a root trusted by the consumer;"],[2449,"its dates and usages allow the operation;"],[2508,"Its public certificate in `ca.pem`; this example uses the root created in the previous path."],[2447,"its public key matches the client's private key;"],[2569,"Its public key matches the one included in `device.crt`."],[348,"Its status contains the expected address."],[2446,"its subject and extensions identify the expected client;"],[25,"Just want to evaluate the platform?"],[1458,"Just-In-Time Provisioning (JITP) mode delegates provisioning to AWS instead of the connector doing it directly. When the connector detects the DMS is in `jitp` mode, it creates a JITP provisioning template in AWS IoT Core and attaches it to the registered CA certificate. The connector attempts to create this template when the DMS is created and again on every full DMS edit (`PUT /v1/dms/{id}`) — not at device enrollment time. The attempt is not idempotent: when a template named after the DMS ID already exists, the creation call fails, the event is retried three times and finally lands in the `lamassu-dlq` poison queue (see Troubleshooting). The template is created once; later DMS edits never update its content. A DMS update that omits `metadata` or sends `null` keeps the stored metadata, so the connector key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}` remains present and the attempt is still triggered. A body that supplies a `metadata` object replaces the stored metadata entirely: if that object does not carry the connector key, the key is dropped and the connector silently skips the DMS's future events. Once the template is attached, when a device with a certificate issued by that CA connects to AWS IoT Core for the first time, AWS executes the template automatically: it creates the Thing, registers the certificate and activates it."],[283,"K3s"],[305,"K3s ServiceLB implements each `LoadBalancer` Service with pods that reserve the Service ports on the node. On a single node, only one Service can therefore own port 80 and one can own port 443. The default K3s Traefik Service may already hold both ports."],[640,"Keep `failOpen: false` unless a documented risk assessment justifies otherwise."],[267,"Keep `migrations.caToKms.enabled: true` for the upgrade that performs the migration, then remove the flag from your values once the migration completes; it defaults to `false`."],[925,"Keep `true` to verify CSR key possession."],[642,"Keep at least two recovery administrators and test their access periodically."],[2138,"Keep enough overlap to renew a disconnected fleet."],[1264,"Keep initial EST server trust separate from identity consumer trust."],[758,"keep the complete CloudEvent unmodified;"],[1707,"Keep the device identifier, owning DMS and serial number as references for the result."],[1161,"Keep the result as `device.next.crt` and `device.next.key` until verified. Switch the pair through a coordinated operation in the device store or configuration; avoid temporarily exposing a certificate with another version's key."],[2134,"Keep the root out of daily operations, or with very restricted access."],[2014,"Keep the serial number, issuer, revocation reason, query time and evidence timestamps. An existing session may need explicit closure or reauthentication in the consumer; publishing revocation does not automatically close it."],[299,"Keep these two settings separate:"],[2587,"Key"],[1716,"Key and certificate"],[781,"Key custody and cryptographic operations"],[1771,"Key custody, CA hierarchy, profiles and validation."],[2262,"Key inventory"],[1786,"Key rotation procedure"],[1919,"Key Usage and Extended Key Usage"],[2252,"Keys and cryptographic engines"],[748,"keys and KMS engines;"],[1747,"Keys, authorities, profiles, certificates and publication of revocation status."],[410,"KMS deployments using the native PKCS#11 sidecar require Kubernetes 1.29 or newer. Prebuilt Lamassu container images are published for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`, and the KMS PKCS#11 client module (`p11-kit-client.so`) is installed at the target architecture's Debian library path: `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on amd64 and `/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on arm64. The x86\\_64 path is retained as a compatibility alias for existing configurations."],[1992,"KU, EKU and constraints permit the specific operation."],[404,"Kubernetes 1.24 or newer"],[2796,"Kubernetes derives a StatefulSet's PVC name from its pod name (`<volumeClaimTemplate>-<pod-name>`). Renaming the `kms` and `va` StatefulSets means `helm upgrade` binds brand-new, empty PVCs instead of the existing ones. The KMS key material and VA CRLs are not deleted — the old PVCs are orphaned, not removed — but the upgraded pods fail with `no such file or directory` (KMS) or `code=NotFound` (VA) until you copy the data across. This affects KMS when `services.kms.cryptoEngines` uses a `filesystem` engine and VA when `services.va.fileStore.type` is `local`. Every other renamed resource is stateless and is recreated without data-loss risk."],[32,"Kubernetes self-managed"],[354,"Kubernetes self-managed"],[956,"Label used in service logs."],[42,"Lamassu"],[1126,"Lamassu `JITP` creates inventory and identity in Lamassu. AWS JITP provisions a destination in AWS; these are separate decisions."],[2681,"Lamassu 4.0.0 is a major release. Storage consolidates into a single PostgreSQL database, a new `authz` service authorizes every request, the Helm chart is hardened, and several APIs and stored configurations change shape. Most data converts automatically during the upgrade, but some changes need operator action before, during or after it."],[526,"Lamassu access control turns an authenticated identity into concrete permissions. The OIDC provider or the client certificate proves **who** makes a request; the `authz` service decides **what that identity can do** and **over which resources**."],[2372,"Lamassu also offers specific help for local integrations through **Sign locally with OpenSSL & PKCS11 tools**."],[376,"Lamassu applications"],[2282,"Lamassu can work with different cryptographic engines depending on deployment requirements. In some environments a software engine is enough; in others custody must be delegated to an HSM, a cloud service or a specialized platform."],[140,"Lamassu chart version"],[779,"Lamassu components and how control, keys, issuance and devices relate."],[796,"Lamassu connects three elements: a **root of trust**, an **issuance policy** and an **entity that needs to prove its identity**. The platform coordinates these elements throughout the certificate lifecycle."],[678,"Lamassu delivers the event to the configured channel."],[785,"Lamassu deploys as a set of independent services. This separation keeps every component from needing direct access to private keys and lets each capability scale according to load."],[1809,"Lamassu does not allow reissuing an already-revoked CA or a CA whose expiration date has passed. Plan ahead for both conditions."],[527,"Lamassu does not assign permissions directly to users. The full chain is:"],[676,"Lamassu evaluates the subscriptions associated with that event type."],[738,"Lamassu instruments the mutable operations of its services with audit events. Each event describes the operation, the principals that matched, the input, the result and whether it ended in error."],[1738,"Lamassu IoT lets you administer a private PKI and use it to issue, renew and revoke X.509 device identities. The Platform documentation follows two paths: **PKI**, for authorities and certificates, and **IoT Fleets**, for applying those identities to a device population."],[1725,"Lamassu IoT Platform"],[1913,"Lamassu keeps the certificate record of a deleted CA. If that same certificate is imported again, the import succeeds and reuses the record: it updates the type, issuer and key but keeps the status, so the CA reappears in the state it had when deleted (normally revoked or expired). A different certificate with the same serial number is rejected with a 400 error, and a certificate that already belongs to an existing CA with a 409 error. To avoid inheriting the previous status, use a certificate with a different serial number and check the result after importing. The reimported CA is registered under a new, backend-generated CA ID and does not inherit the CRL settings of the deleted CA: until those settings are deleted, their last CRL keeps being served at the CA ID URL, and at `/crl/<ski>` — the URL that certificates issued before the 4.0.0 upgrade carry — when the settings were migrated by that upgrade; archived settings can no longer be edited (see CRL)."],[805,"Lamassu keeps the inventory, history and status of the identity."],[2075,"Lamassu persists four X.509 states:"],[1390,"Lamassu resource"],[54,"Lamassu SaaS offers the platform's capabilities in a managed environment. It is the right option when your team wants to focus on PKI and device identities, not on maintaining the infrastructure that supports them."],[1820,"Lamassu saves the CA’s revocation, then attempts to revoke child CAs and issued certificates with `CessationOfOperation`. Propagation continues recursively through child CAs. This is not a transaction over the whole hierarchy: some resources may have changed while other updates failed."],[368,"Lamassu services and Gateway API resources"],[2292,"Lamassu shows the enabled engines in the console."],[2191,"Lamassu takes the SKI of an imported certificate as-is and does not check that it corresponds to its public key. Until Lamassu rejects imports whose SKI collides with an existing CA — proposed in lamassuiot#834, together with the per-CA CRL that 4.0.0 implements — restrict the permission to import CAs as an interim control; it covers only the import path. Creating a CA with &#x2A;*Create New CA (Existing Key)** on a KMS key another CA already uses produces the same SKI collision, so avoid reusing keys across CAs."],[535,"Lamassu uses two authorization paths depending on who implements the protected service. They share principals, policies and grants, but they do not interpret the rules the same way."],[2620,"lamassu-dashboard"],[2621,"lamassu-helm"],[1275,"Lamassu's connector for AWS IoT Core syncs device identities with the AWS platform, handling CA registration, Thing provisioning and certificate lifecycle automatically and event-driven."],[1301,"Lamassu's integration with AWS IoT Core bridges the PKI managed by Lamassu and AWS's connectivity platform. When a device obtains its certificate through th