t and certificate response formats. Authentication comes from the independent `reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` block. With a client certificate, its CN must match the CSR CN; the subject is also checked against the current certificate."],[845,"Use this flow to prepare inventory or assign an identity through the console. You need an existing DMS."],[2039,"Use this flow when the identity’s key must remain on the system that generated it. You need an active CA with a usable key, a PEM CSR and a suitable profile."],[293,"Usually, no. If you omit it, the Gateway controller and the cluster load-balancer implementation can assign an address and report it in `Gateway.status.addresses` and the generated Service status."],[2669,"VA CRL signer"],[2772,"VA CRL signer"],[2401,"VA service path"],[1166,"Valid certificate, outside the window"],[1986,"Valid chain to a root deliberately installed in the consumer."],[2139,"Validate from the consuming system, not only from the Lamassu console."],[422,"Validate the result from outside"],[182,"validates local tools and the selected cluster"],[2107,"Validation and revocation"],[2679,"Validation checklist"],[2686,"Validation checklist: how to confirm the upgrade worked before you remove the old data."],[2444,"Validation covers the chain, validity, usages and revocation status. Lamassu publishes status through OCSP and CRL; the consumer configures which issuers it recognizes and how it checks that status."],[491,"validation from a consumer;"],[751,"validation functions and CRL;"],[1917,"Validity"],[2536,"Validity fails"],[240,"Variables"],[2270,"Verify"],[1268,"Verify chain, dates, names, usages and revocation from the consumer."],[1627,"Verify CSR Signature"],[442,"verify issuer, audience and expiration of the token;"],[473,"verify it is enabled in the emitting service;"],[449,"verify that authz reaches PostgreSQL and its JWKS."],[2502,"Verify the chain"],[1240,"Verify the CSR locally and relate its CN to the inventory."],[1679,"Verify the device"],[454,"Verify the DNS and port configured in externalAuthorization."],[896,"Verify the endpoints"],[1847,"Verify the import"],[89,"Verify the installation"],[1606,"Verify the obtained identity"],[817,"Verify the registration"],[2040,"Verify the request with `openssl req -in request.csr -noout -verify -text`. Review subject, SAN and algorithm, and authorize the requested identity."],[2500,"Verify the result"],[2462,"Verify the result [!toc]"],[2414,"Verify the returned DER:"],[290,"Verify the traffic path"],[1982,"Verify these criteria together:"],[2693,"Version 4.0.0 of the chart is a security-hardening release:"],[57,"Version upgrades."],[263,"Version-specific upgrade steps live in `charts/lamassu/CHANGELOG/` in the Helm repository. The chart also ships an optional pre-upgrade Job that migrates existing CA private keys into the KMS engine configured for the release. It is off by default (`migrations.caToKms.enabled: false`) and is only needed when upgrading from a version prior to 3.7.0."],[2162,"Version, validity start and end of the last published list. Read-only."],[2267,"View details"],[2560,"We use `Acme Evaluation Root CA` and `device-001` as example names. If you replace them, keep the same client identifier in the steps you want to connect."],[944,"Webhook and combined modes also execute during re-enrollment when configured in its block. They do not implicitly degrade to NO\\_AUTH. An absent or unrecognized mode fails."],[870,"Webhook contract"],[979,"Webhook credentials must be stored as PEM content. File references (`ca_cert_file`, `mtls_options.cert_file`, `mtls_options.key_file`) are never valid in a stored webhook config: delivery rejects any webhook config that references files, whatever its auth mode, so every enroll and reenroll that calls that webhook fails until the certificate and key are entered as Base64-encoded or plain PEM in `mtls_options.cert_content` and `key_content`, or the file fields are removed if the webhook does not use mTLS. If a stored config holds file paths in `mtls_options.cert_content` or `key_content`, every full settings update (`POST /v1/dms`, `PUT /v1/dms/:id`) fails validation — settings are replaced wholesale, so the stored config must be resent — until the values are replaced by PEM content or the webhook stops being used. Meanwhile, delivery of that webhook fails on every call; a metadata-only update through `PUT` or `PATCH` on `/v1/dms/:id/metadata` still succeeds. To fix it in the console, open the DMS, replace any file-path value with PEM content — the editor rejects file paths with an inline field error — and save. Saving rewrites `external_webhook_settings.config` with the current keys, and the console reads both plain and Base64-encoded PEM from migrated configurations and stores the material Base64-encoded. If your stored config previously relied on `validate_server_cert: false`, it now carries `insecure_skip_verify: true`: receiver-certificate verification stays skipped until you set that flag to `false`. See 3.8.0 to 4.0.0 for the upgrade notes and the operator checklist."],[671,"Webhook delivery rules"],[1202,"Webhook denies, times out or responds without authorization"],[2736,"Webhooks with file references"],[1916,"What a profile controls"],[727,"What an event contains"],[1248,"What cacerts returns"],[2102,"What can be issued"],[2637,"What changes in the 4.0.0 architecture, which changes break a 3.8.0 deployment, how to upgrade a Helm release step by step and how to validate the result."],[2841,"What changes is how API requests are handled. Bodies that still carry `additional_validation_cas` are accepted, but the value is silently dropped because unknown JSON fields are ignored, and GET responses no longer include it. An update body built from the old layout therefore empties `validation_cas` without any error. Always read the DMS before sending an edit and reuse the layout the API returns."],[80,"What Fastlane adds"],[286,"What if you need three Gateways?"],[365,"What it installs"],[1769,"What it prepares or checks"],[1567,"What it represents"],[1298,"What the connector writes to the shadow"],[2254,"What the KMS solves"],[358,"What the Lamassu chart provides"],[86,"What the script configures"],[51,"What the service manages"],[2643,"What the upgrade migrates automatically"],[1116,"What to check"],[1193,"What to check"],[2613,"What to include in a report"],[2582,"What to inspect"],[2529,"What to review"],[1744,"What you manage"],[2000,"What you must maintain"],[2425,"What you need"],[2068,"What you should watch"],[2702,"When `migrations.caToKms.enabled` is set, a pre-upgrade hook registers legacy CA keys in the KMS."],[1447,"When `primary_account` is `false`, the connector registers the CA using `CertificateMode: SNI_ONLY`. This mode requires no verification code or access to the CA's private key, so it is the right choice when the CA is managed from another AWS account or when the administrator does not hold the private key. It is the usual method to register the same CA in several AWS accounts simultaneously."],[2179,"When a CA is reissued and the signer is the superseded CA certificate, the VA moves the signer to the new certificate — provided it carries `cRLSign` — and republishes the CRL. If the new certificate lacks `cRLSign`, the VA keeps the previous signer and logs a warning. If the signer is any other certificate with the CA’s subject and key, it is deliberately left unchanged; the previous certificate then cannot be deleted while the settings use it (the API returns `409` `certificate is in use`), and to retire it you update the signer with `PUT`."],[2320,"When an existing PKI needs to be migrated without reissuing certificates."],[2820,"When CA certificates sharing a `subject_key_id` declare conflicting `engine_id`s, the tool fails that key and exits with code `1`, which fails a Helm pre-upgrade hook. Resolve the conflict manually before rerunning."],[2609,"When escalating a problem, provide identifiers, error and relevant public evidence, including query timestamps and version. This distinguishes issuance, publication and consumer application failures."],[547,"When it allows access, `authz` returns the selected principal in the `X-Principal-ID` response header. The Gateway can propagate that header to the service to preserve the identity context. Clients cannot forge this header: the Gateway removes client-supplied `X-Principal-Id` (and the other forwarded identity and certificate headers) before forwarding, so only `authz` can set it. See Expose the Gateway for the full list of stripped headers."],[1948,"When it is enabled, Lamassu checks the public key before creating a CA or signing a CSR. A key whose type or size is not listed in the profile is rejected."],[2321,"When Lamassu must operate with a root of trust created by a third party."],[1307,"When several connectors run in parallel, for example for different AWS accounts, each instance has its own `connector_id`. All metadata the connector writes to Lamassu is stored under the key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}`, allowing coexistence without collisions."],[503,"When several principals match"],[1441,"When the `primary_account` parameter is `true`, the connector runs the full registration flow with proof-of-possession of the private key:"],[1519,"When the connector marks a certificate as `REVOKED` or `INACTIVE` in AWS, it additionally performs a forced disconnect of the device. To do so it opens an MQTT connection over WebSocket (with SigV4 authentication using the connector's credentials) to the AWS IoT Core data endpoint, using the certificate's CN as the MQTT client identifier, and closes it immediately. This terminates any active MQTT session the device may have at that moment."],[1486,"When the device establishes its first MQTT connection to AWS IoT Core presenting the Lamassu-issued certificate, AWS detects that the certificate belongs to a CA registered with a JITP template, executes that template and creates the Thing automatically. The initial connection may fail or be delayed while the template runs; firmware must be prepared to retry the connection."],[1115,"When the device exists"],[2836,"When the legacy mTLS certificate and key are both PEM, they move into `mtls_options.cert_content` and `mtls_options.key_content` and need no action. Two cases do need it."],[2848,"When the request `Accept` header includes `text/event-stream`, the events endpoint streams over SSE with a keepalive every 15 seconds, and returns `501` when SSE is disabled in the deployment. Each Device Manager instance binds a durable AMQP queue named after its service ID, `device-manager-sse-<uuid>`. Queues from crashed or replaced instances linger until removed, so configure an expiration or cleanup policy on the broker."],[1785,"When to choose each operation"],[1999,"When to use it"],[639,"When you edit a shared policy, remember the change affects all its principals. To test a new scope, create a separate policy, grant it to a test identity and validate allowed and denied operations before replacing the old one."],[2319,"When you have an exportable private key and the destination engine supports import."],[2342,"When you open it, two areas appear: **Sign** and **Verify**."],[2362,"When you open the form, it asks for the subject data and the main certificate attributes:"],[76,"When you receive your instance's credentials, continue with Get started."],[1394,"Where in this page"],[264,"While the flag is enabled, every `helm upgrade` creates the `<chart fullname>-ca-to-kms-migration` Job (`lamassu-ca-to-kms-migration` for release `lamassu`); a fresh install does not create it. Its hook weight is `-10`, so it runs before the other pre-upgrade hooks: the authorization bootstrap configuration (weight `-4`) and the database-migration Job (weight `-3`)."],[2104,"Who accepts the result"],[2101,"Who can issue"],[2103,"Who can request it"],[2705,"Who is affected"],[30,"Who operates the infrastructure"],[1562,"Who prepares each part"],[1941,"With **Honor Extensions**, Lamassu filters the additional requested extensions and keeps only SAN. Without that option, it discards the CSR's additional extensions."],[1940,"With **Honor Subject**, the certificate keeps the subject of the CSR. Without that option, Lamassu applies the profile's `CN`, `O`, `OU`, `C`, `ST` and `L`. If the profile does not define a `Common Name`, the requested CN is kept."],[179,"With `--development`, the fixed `lamassu`/`lamassu` password grants `pki-admin` access to anyone who can reach the installation. Never use it on anything exposed beyond a development machine or a network-reachable host."],[875,"With `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE`, the device presents its initial certificate and proves key possession through TLS. The DMS validates it against admission CAs. In the inspected flow, the validator receives one leaf credential: configure and test the direct issuer for the simple case; sending more intermediates does not establish delivery to the service."],[2199,"With `regenerate_on_revoke`, the event handler requests a new CRL upon revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` to `ACTIVE`. This happens after the status change and has its own failure paths: if generation fails (for example, because of an invalid signer), the event is retried and, once retries are exhausted, moves to the failed-events queue. If the issuing CA has no CRL settings, there is no CRL to regenerate: the event is acknowledged without retry, the VA logs a warning, and nothing appears in the failed-events queue. Confirming revocation does not establish that the new CRL is already published or that all clients have downloaded it."],[2808,"With `tls.certManagerOptions.issuer` empty, the chart creates a release-scoped self-signed issuer, for example `lamassu-downstream-ca-selfsigned-issuer`. The legacy literal `downstream-ca-selfsigned-issuer` is still accepted and resolves to it."],[192,"With both `--tls-crt` and `--tls-key`, Fastlane creates the `downstream-provided-crt` Secret and configures `tls.type: external`."],[1169,"With certificate authentication, review `enable_expired_renewal` and whether Gateway permits presenting that credential. Revocation, subject and the remaining checks still apply."],[977,"With credentials configured, they are sent regardless of the URL scheme: an `http://` webhook receives the API key or bearer token in cleartext, and mTLS has no effect because no TLS handshake takes place, so the client certificate is never presented. Prefer `https://` for `url`, `oidc_well_known` and the token endpoint it advertises."],[2823,"With Helm, enable the hook for the upgrade in step 2; without Helm, run the tool by hand."],[1027,"With initial `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE` authentication, send the Base64-encoded DER CSR using `Content-Type: application/pkcs10`:"],[233,"With no CA Secret configured, the event-bus clients use the system-trusted CAs. To trust a private CA, create a Secret in the release namespace containing the CA certificate in PEM format and set `amqp.ca.secretName` (and `amqp.ca.certificateKey` if the data key is not `ca.crt`)."],[2901,"With release `lamassu` and the default chart name, the new PVCs are `golang-engine-storage-lamassu-kms-0` and `local-crl-file-storage-lamassu-va-0`; with release `prod` they are `golang-engine-storage-prod-lamassu-kms-0` and `local-crl-file-storage-prod-lamassu-va-0`. Confirm both old and new PVCs exist before continuing. If `claims_ok` prints `ABORT`, the block stops before listing the claims: either a StatefulSet lookup failed, or a new claim resolves to the 3.8.0 claim. In the second case the copy would verify against itself and the final cleanup would delete the live volume. The chart always appends `-kms` and `-va` to the release-scoped name, so a collision means the claims are not what this guide expects; investigate before going on. Item 3 runs the same check and refuses to create the helper pod, so nothing touches the volumes. The check returns instead of calling `exit`, which would close your shell and lose the variables set so far. If you only migrate one of the two services, drop the other one's claims from the commands that follow."],[1658,"With the same server still running, test that a request without a client certificate is rejected:"],[2912,"Without the chart's database job, no tool moves data between databases: the migration tooling only applies migrations to a schema that already exists. Move each per-service database into its `pki` schema yourself, preserving structure, indexes, constraints, data and migration history, with writes stopped and a verified backup in hand."],[193,"Without them, it configures cert-manager with a self-signed issuer. This is suitable for a lab but will not be trusted by clients until you distribute the CA. Use Helm when you need a corporate Issuer, public ACME issuer or an existing Secret managed by another system."],[363,"Workflow"],[459,"wrong credentials or DNS for PostgreSQL or RabbitMQ;"],[502,"X.509 principals"],[1341,"Yes"],[1345,"Yes"],[1349,"Yes"],[1373,"Yes"],[1377,"Yes"],[1381,"Yes"],[1385,"Yes"],[2435,"You can complete that path before managing devices. When you need to associate the identity with an inventory or automate its enrollment, continue in IoT Fleets."],[1934,"You can express the lifetime as a duration from the moment of issuance or as a fixed end date. A duration works well for recurring identities; a common date is useful to make a whole batch end before a migration or retirement."],[1901,"You can filter the inventory by name, status and CA type."],[27,"You can run Lamassu on your own Kubernetes cluster, deploy a prebuilt image from AWS Marketplace or consume the platform as a managed service."],[1902,"You can start an issuance from **Issued Certificates**, from a CA's action menu or from the global certificate inventory."],[1173,"You cannot prove possession of the previous identity. Authorize new admission and, if appropriate